   "source": [
    "# 44. Why is Ridge Regression called Ridge?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "c8c4f4ca",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Why is Ridge Regression called “Ridge”?\n",
    "\n",
    "Ridge Regression is called “Ridge” because of the shape formed in the optimization landscape when the L2 regularization penalty is added.\n",
    "\n",
    "The name comes from:\n",
    "\n",
    "Ridge-like stabilization in the parameter estimation process\n",
    "Especially when dealing with multicollinearity\n",
    "📌 Ridge Regression Formula\n",
    "\n",
    "Loss=RSS+λ∑wi^2\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "RSS = Residual Sum of Squares\n",
    "λ = regularization strength\n",
    "wi^2 = L2 penalty\n",
    "🧠 Historical/Geometric Intuition\n",
    "\n",
    "In ordinary linear regression:\n",
    "\n",
    "Coefficients can become unstable when features are highly correlated.\n",
    "\n",
    "Adding the L2 penalty:\n",
    "\n",
    "Creates a ridge constraint\n",
    "Smooths the optimization surface\n",
    "Prevents coefficients from becoming excessively large\n",
    "📊 Geometry Behind the Name\n",
    "\n",
    "The L2 constraint forms a circular/spherical boundary around coefficients.\n",
    "\n",
    "This creates a “ridge” in the optimization space that:\n",
    "\n",
    "Stabilizes the solution\n",
    "Shrinks coefficients smoothly\n",
    "🎯 Main Purpose of Ridge Regression\n",
    "\n",
    "✔️ Reduce overfitting\n",
    "✔️ Handle multicollinearity\n",
    "✔️ Improve generalization\n",
    "\n",
    "⚠️ Important Point\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 Ridge Regression:\n",
    "\n",
    "Shrinks coefficients toward zero\n",
    "But does not make them exactly zero\n",
    "\n",
    "(Unlike Lasso)\n",
    "\n",
    "🎯 Interview-Ready Answer (Perfect)**\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 “Ridge Regression is called ‘Ridge’ because the L2 regularization term creates a ridge-like constraint in the optimization landscape, stabilizing coefficient estimates and reducing the effect of multicollinearity by shrinking large coefficients.”\n",
    "\n",
    "🔥 Pro Tip\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 Say this to impress interviewer:\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 “Ridge regression adds an L2 penalty that smooths the loss surface and stabilizes the solution.”"
   ]
  },
